Q: Multiple search-and-replace inside the same text A: <p><strong><em>Updated - Added in a speed test using a split() method.</em></strong><br />
This involves splitting the text along the simple regex pattern parts<br />
<code>/(regtext\d+|regtext[A-Z]+)/</code> thereby ignoring the interstitial parts.<br />
A single pass is made to modify / clear elements, then finally all the parts<br />
are joined to form a new string.<br />
The speed appears linear 0(n) with respect to the number of replacements.</p>
<p><strong><em>Updated - Speed tests added at the end.</em></strong><br />
These tests are inconclusive as to which way is better, but note that it can't be<br />
done by starting over at the beginning of the target each time,<br />
in a while loop. This is many times (if not thousands of times) slower.<br />
And with a very large data set, time to get some coffee.</p>
<p>It could be done using the best of both worlds where
there is a loop around the substitution regex that starts anew
after/from the last recorded match (see regex comments how this is calculated).</p>
<p>This encompasses @BhuvaneswariBalan and @ikegami methods.
It is really a single pass regex leveraging <em><code>pos()</code></em> and the <em><code>\G</code></em> anchor,<br />
in a while loop and non-global.</p>
<p>This is a Single pass regex, Perl code demo  <a href="https://www.jdoodle.com/ia/1RAK" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.jdoodle.com/ia/1RAK</a> :</p>
<pre><code>use strict;
use warnings;

my $cp = 0; 

my $inp = &quot;text regtext1 text regtext2 text regtextA regtextZ end&quot;;
my $imod = $inp;

while ( $imod =~ s/\G.*?\K(reg)(text\d+(?{$cp=pos()}).*?)(regtext[A-Z]+)(?{$cp+=length($^N)})/$1$3$2/s ) {
    pos($imod) = $cp;
}

print ( &quot;\n$inp\n&quot;, $imod, &quot;\n&quot; );
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>text regtext1 text regtext2 text regtextA regtextZ end
text regregtextAtext1 text regregtextZtext2 text   end
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><em><strong>Speed tests</strong></em> :<br />
Includes a split() method benchmark for comparison (tens of thousands times faster).<br />
The @sln and @ikigami tests were suspended after the first <em><code>5000</code></em> sample.<br />
The @split tests were done for all the sample ranges.</p>
<pre><code>use strict;
use warnings;
use Benchmark ':hireswallclock';

my $X = &quot;text regtext1 text regtext2 text regtext3 text regtext4 text regtextA regtextB regtextC regtextZ end&quot;;
my ($t0,$t1,$inp,$s,$p,$cp);

my @sampcnts = ( 5000, 7500, 10000, 15000, 20000, 25000, 30000 );
foreach my $samp (@sampcnts)
{
    $inp = &quot;&quot;;

    for my $cnt (1 .. $samp) {
        $inp .= $X;
    }
    $s = $inp;
    $p = $inp;
    $cp = 0;

    print (&quot;\n-----------------\nSample count of lines = &quot;, $samp,  &quot;\n\n&quot; );


    #########################

    $t0 = new Benchmark;

    my @parts = split ( /(regtext\d+|regtext[A-Z]+)/, $p  );
    print ( &quot;Size parts = &quot;, scalar( @parts) , &quot;\n\n&quot;);

    my ( $out, $in, $found ) = (0,0, 0);  # out = leading pos, in = trailing pos

    for ( $out = 0; $out &lt; @parts; $out++ )  # traverse the parts array
    {
        last if ( $in &gt;= @parts );

        if ( $parts[ $out ] =~ /(reg)(text\d+)/ )   # find the next substitution what
        {
            if ( $in &lt;= $out ) { $in = $out+1; }  # inner search pos should be &gt; outter pos

            my ($D1,$D2) = ($1,$2);         # save parts to be substituted

            for ( ; $in &lt; @parts; $in++ )   # traverse parts array
            {
                if ( $parts[ $in ] =~ /(regtext[A-Z]+)/ )
                {
                    $parts[ $out ] = &quot;$D1$1$D2&quot;;
                    $parts[ $in ] = &quot;&quot;;
                    $found = 1;
                    last;
                }
             }
        }
    }
    if ( $found )  # construct a new string from modified parts
    {
        $p = &quot;&quot;;
        for ( my $i = 0; $i &lt; @parts; $i++ )
        {
            $p .= $parts[ $i ];
        }
    }
    $t1 = new Benchmark;
    print &quot;   Regex \@split     -took: &quot;, timestr(timediff($t1, $t0)), &quot;\n&quot;;

    if ( $ samp == 5000 )  # only do these slow pos() ways once
    {

    #########################

        $t0 = new Benchmark;
        while ( $inp =~ s/\G.*?\K(reg)(text\d+(?{$cp=pos()}).*?)(regtext[A-Z]+)(?{$cp+=length($^N)})/$1$3$2/sx) {
            pos($inp) = $cp;
        }
        $t1 = new Benchmark;
        print &quot;   Regex \@sln       -took: &quot;, timestr(timediff($t1, $t0)), &quot;\n&quot;;


    #############################

        $t0 = new Benchmark;
        while ( $s =~ /reg(text\d+).*?(regtext[A-Z]+)/g ) {
           my $ss_len = $+[2] - $-[2];   my $ss = substr( $s, $-[2], $ss_len, &quot;&quot; );
           substr( $s, $-[1], 0, $ss );
           pos( $s ) = $+[1] + $ss_len;
        }
        $t1 = new Benchmark;
        print &quot;   Regex \@ikegami   -took: &quot;, timestr(timediff($t1, $t0)), &quot;\n\n&quot;;

        if ( $inp eq $p &amp;&amp; $inp eq $s ) 
             { print ( &quot;   Identical substitution result strings\n&quot; ); }
        else { print ( &quot;   Different substitution result strings\n&quot; ); }
    }

}

</code></pre>
